<h1 class="article-title">Port Cities Liepāja and Lübeck Sign Cooperation Memorandum</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>On 11 July, during the German–Latvian Port Days, Liepāja and Lübeck signed a four-party memorandum of cooperation. It was signed by the heads of the two municipalities and representatives of the Port of Lübeck and the Liepāja SEZ Authority. The memorandum sets two principal objectives: reducing CO₂ emissions and developing infrastructure to ensure that port terminals operate efficiently, sustainably and with modern facilities in support of growing cargo flows and passenger traffic. This includes accommodating larger ships, thereby reducing CO₂ emissions per unit of cargo.</p><p>“The cooperation memorandum with the city and port of Lübeck is another step towards making Liepāja an even greener, more environmentally friendly city, in line with Europe’s transition to a green economy. The maritime connection between Liepāja and Lübeck has historically been an important waterway. We are now committed to developing it as an environmentally friendly shipping corridor supporting supply chains across Europe,” says Liepāja Mayor Gunārs Ansiņš.</p><p>An important part of the memorandum is an agreement to cooperate on the continued reconstruction of Berth No. 46 in the Port of Liepāja, ensuring safe mooring and ferry services for larger vessels. Reconstruction of the berth will improve the competitiveness of the Liepāja–Travemünde route and support the development of future routes.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis stresses that both existing and future cooperation with Lübeck and its outer port of Travemünde makes it possible to set and achieve new goals. “Maritime transport is greener and friendlier to the environment and people. We are pleased that the ferry service is performing well and showing positive growth, but many trucks still travel by road. This memorandum provides for cooperation to increase ferry cargo volumes and accommodate larger vessels. We will continue working towards the successful reconstruction and development of Berth No. 46, which serves the ferry route.”</p><p>The amount of cargo carried on the ferry route has increased considerably in recent years. In 2019, five years earlier, the route carried 799,449.67 tonnes of cargo, 49,296 ro-ro units and 39,987 passengers. By 2023, cargo volumes had doubled to 1,599,037.09 tonnes, while ro-ro units reached 97,419 and passenger numbers 93,777. In the first half of 2024, ro-ro cargo on the Liepāja–Travemünde ferry route also accounted for the largest increase in cargo throughput at the Port of Liepāja, at 14.16%.</p><p>“The ferry terminal demonstrates how public and private investment in infrastructure development can increase cargo throughput. Just as important are timely risk management to minimise disruption to cargo flows, attracting local cargo through close cooperation between the port and manufacturing companies, and strong collaboration among businesses, the Liepāja SEZ Authority, state institutions and the municipality,” says Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis.</p></div>
